{
  "dom_id": "question-342023",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "Let $f(x)$ be the $4$-periodic extension of the function $F(x)=8x$ defined on $[-2,2).$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of a series converging to the $4$-periodic extension of \n\n$$\nG(x)=4{x}^{2}={\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nfrom the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$\n\nThe Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n8x∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{32(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }{{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is \n{{field-2}} \non $R,$ we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{{field-3}}\\cdot {{field-4}}.\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
